                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Dawn of a Soul is a small puzzle game that caught me off guard with how much thought it expects from me. The main idea is to build chains and combos across a board while trying to reach a target score with a limited number of moves. It sounds simple at first, but the game is really about finding the right path rather than just matching things together. I have to look at the board, work out where my chain should begin and then figure out how to use the available pieces without wasting my moves.\n\nWhat I like most is how the puzzles gradually make me think differently. There are 64 handcrafted puzzles and several different areas of focus, with new puzzle elements being introduced as I progress. The game does not rely on complicated controls, so most of the challenge comes from understanding the layout and recognising patterns. I like that because the difficulty feels based on actually thinking about what I am doing rather than trying to beat a timer or deal with awkward controls.\n\nThe chain system is probably the strongest part for me. I have quite a lot of control over how I create my path, and getting the right combination can feel really satisfying when I finally see how the pieces fit together. Some puzzles are straightforward enough that I can work them out quickly, while others make me stop and reconsider what I thought the solution was. There is also a nice feeling when I find a more efficient route after initially making a mess of the board. It makes the simple mechanics feel more rewarding than they first appear.\n\nThe presentation is very minimal, but I think that works in its favour. The dark fantasy setting and strange atmosphere give the game some personality without distracting me from the puzzles. The visuals are not particularly detailed, but I do not really need them to be. This is a game where I am spending most of my attention looking at patterns and planning my next move. The fact that it is mouse focused also makes it very easy for me to pick up and play for a few minutes whenever I feel like doing something that requires a bit of concentration.\n\nThe biggest weakness for me is that the experience is quite limited outside of the puzzles themselves. There is not much of a traditional story or character development to keep me moving forward, and once I understand the main mechanics, the game is almost entirely about solving the remaining puzzles. That is not necessarily a problem for a puzzle game, but it means the quality of the individual puzzles has to carry everything. There are also times when I know what I want to achieve but have to experiment a little before understanding exactly how the game expects me to use a particular element.\n\nI also think the difficulty curve could be a little smoother in places. Some puzzles feel like a natural step up from what I have already learned, while others suddenly make me rethink the whole way I approach the board. I actually enjoy having difficult puzzles, but the difference between a puzzle I solve quickly and one that completely stops me can be quite noticeable. Still, I would rather have a puzzle game that makes me sit and think than one that gives me solutions so quickly that I barely remember them afterwards.\n\nOverall, Dawn of a Soul is a simple but clever puzzle game that I think does a good job with its central idea. The 64 puzzles, chain building, limited moves and gradual introduction of new elements give me enough to think about without making the controls complicated. The lack of deeper content and the uneven difficulty stop it from reaching the higher ratings I give to my favourite puzzle games, but I like how focused it is and I appreciate that the game trusts me to work things out for myself. It is also free to play, which makes the package even easier for me to recommend when I want a short and thoughtful puzzle experience.\n\nRating: 7/10\n",
